<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Manifest of the **debug** variant of `:phone`. It did not exist: until now `src/debug/` carried
  only code (the `@Preview` previews and the `TimeScaling` twin), that is, nothing requiring a
  system declaration.

  ### What it declares, and why it is here and not in `src/main/`

  A single `BroadcastReceiver`: the bench seeding trigger. It fills the database with synthetic
  nights, and **that must exist in no publishable build**. Putting it in `src/main/` behind an
  `if (BuildConfig.DEBUG)` guard would not be enough: the class and its manifest entry would go
  into the release APK all the same, where anybody can read them and invoke them. The source set is
  the only barrier that rests on no vigilance — the release variant simply does not compile this
  directory, and `aapt dump xmltree` on the release APK does not find the receiver.

  It is the same reasoning that moved `PreviewData` into `src/debug`, and the same guarantee
  `:sleepwriter` obtains by disabling its release variant.

  ### `exported="true"`

  Imposed by the usage: it is `adb shell am broadcast` that reaches it, so an outside process (the
  shell). A non-exported receiver would be refused with `Permission Denial`, and no signature
  permission repairs that for the shell. The risk is nil where this file exists — a debug APK,
  never signed with the publication key, never distributed.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <application>

        <!--
          The name is **absolute**. A relative name (`.bench.SeederReceiver`) would resolve against
          `applicationId` — `com.pendulum` — and not against the `namespace` `com.pendulum.phone`:
          it is the same rule the main manifest states for all its declarations.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name="com.pendulum.phone.bench.SeederReceiver"
            android:exported="true" />

    </application>
</manifest>
